Title: Name downloaded files from DOI metadata and flag title mismatches
Created: 2026-09-23
Author: haduong

--- log ---
2026-09-23T12:33Z haduong created

--- body ---
## Context

DOIfetch names each downloaded file from the `--title` the caller passes
(`fetch_istex.py`, l. 66–67: `safe_title = title if title else f"Unknown_{doi}"`).
It never checks that title against the DOI. When the caller passes a wrong title, the file gets a
wrong name and nothing warns.

Observed on 2026-09-23. An agent fetched DOI 10.1016/j.worlddev.2004.03.004 through ISTEX. That DOI is
Stern (2004), "The Rise and Fall of the Environmental Kuznets Curve", World Development 32(8). The
download is correct: its first page carries that DOI. But the file was saved as
`Stern_2004_Development_Discounting_Climate_Change.pdf`, a title the agent supplied. The misnamed file
was then attached in Zotero, and it was caught only because the name looked odd next to the citation.
Without a title, the name is `Unknown_<doi>.pdf`, which is uninformative. Stern, Gerlagh and Burke
(2017) came out as `Unknown_10.1017S1355770X17000109.pdf` in the same session.

The cause is the caller's wrong title. DOIfetch does not pick titles. The gap is that DOIfetch has the
DOI and could name the file from the DOI's own metadata, or at least flag a mismatch.

## Relevant files
- `fetch_istex.py`, `fetch_crossref.py`, `fetch_unpaywall.py`, `fetch_hal.py`, `fetch_ezproxy.py`: each
  builds `file_name` from the passed title.
- `fetch_crossref.py`: already queries Crossref; its metadata call can be reused.
- `utils.py`: shared helpers.

## Actions
1. When a DOI is known, fetch its Crossref metadata once and build the default name as
   `<FirstAuthor> <Year> - <Title>`, so no fetcher needs `Unknown_<doi>`.
2. When the caller also passes `--title`, compare it with the Crossref title using a normalized
   similarity measure. On a clear mismatch, log a warning and name the file from Crossref.
   Keep an option to force the caller's name.
3. Optionally, check that the downloaded PDF's first pages contain the DOI, and log when they do not.

## Test
- Red: fetching the Stern 2004 DOI with `--title "Stern 2004 - Development Discounting Climate
  Change"` produces a file named from Crossref and logs a title-mismatch warning. Mock the network
  calls.
- A DOI fetched without a title gets an author-year-title name, not `Unknown_…`.

## Invariants
- Behavior with a matching title is unchanged.
- Offline or Crossref failure falls back to the current naming, with a logged note.

## Exit criteria
- [ ] Default file names come from DOI metadata
- [ ] A title mismatch is detected and logged, and the file is named from metadata
- [ ] Tests cover both cases; the existing suite passes
